**Sequential driver.**

    long sum = 0;
    LongIterator it = new LongIterator();
    while (it.hasNext()) sum += it.next();

The expected result is n(n+1)/2 = 500,000,500,000, a handy correctness check. Note `long` is needed: the sum overflows `int`.

**Parallel version, the catch.** The iterator keeps mutable state (`val`), and `hasNext()` followed by `next()` is a check-then-act sequence. If two threads call them freely, you can get duplicates, skipped values, or values past `max`. So the pair must be made atomic. A clean approach is each worker keeping a local sum and pulling values under a lock:

    ExecutorService pool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(2);
    Callable<Long> worker = () -> {
        long local = 0;
        while (true) {
            long v;
            synchronized (it) {
                if (!it.hasNext()) break;
                v = it.next();
            }
            local += v;
        }
        return local;
    };
    Future<Long> a = pool.submit(worker), b = pool.submit(worker);
    long total = a.get() + b.get();
    pool.shutdown();

Local sums avoid contention on a shared counter; the final addition is done once. Using a shared `AtomicLong` with `addAndGet` also works, but it is a contended hot spot. `CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(worker, pool)` with `thenCombine(...)` is the same idea.

**Honest trade-off.** Because the source can only be read sequentially, the locked section serialises work and the speed-up may be poor, even negative. Parallelism pays off when the *per-element work* is expensive, or when you can split the source into independent ranges (for example ranges 1..500000 and 500001..1000000), which a plain iterator does not expose. Another design is one reader thread batching values into chunks for workers.

**Why ExecutorService over raw threads:** reuses a bounded pool, returns results and exceptions through futures, and separates task submission from execution policy.
